WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.647
A call-line supervisor at DHS needs to change how callback

00:00:03.647 --> 00:00:07.293
requests are sorted. The usual approach is familiar. Bring a

00:00:07.293 --> 00:00:10.940
draft to the team meeting. Ask for thoughts. Thank everyone.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.586
Move on. It feels inclusive. The research suggests it may

00:00:14.586 --> 00:00:15.680
do almost nothing.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.949
In 2015, researchers studied participative leadership in two settings: workers

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:23.867
in offices and call centers, and factory workers whose output

00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:27.786
was measured directly. They expected that more participation would mean

00:00:27.786 --> 00:00:31.705
better performance. Instead they found a threshold. Below a moderate

00:00:31.705 --> 00:00:35.623
level, participation was not linked to performance at all. Only

00:00:35.623 --> 00:00:39.150
above it did more participation go with better results.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.900
And the effect was strongest when leaders shared a lot

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:44.780
of information.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:48.621
Think about what that means. An occasional 'any thoughts?' may

00:00:48.621 --> 00:00:52.112
never reach the level where participation starts to matter. Asking

00:00:52.112 --> 00:00:55.603
without sharing the data leaves people guessing about limits the

00:00:55.603 --> 00:00:56.650
leader already knows.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.791
A second body of research explains why. A meta-analysis of

00:01:00.791 --> 00:01:04.583
72 studies, covering nearly 4,800 groups, looked at how teams

00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:08.374
share information. Sharing was linked to better performance. But the

00:01:08.374 --> 00:01:12.165
type of sharing mattered. Sharing what only some members knew

00:01:12.165 --> 00:01:15.957
was more strongly linked to performance than simply being open.

00:01:15.957 --> 00:01:19.748
Openness built cohesion. Unique information built results. And left alone,

00:01:19.748 --> 00:01:23.160
groups tend to talk about what everyone already knows.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:26.361
So the supervisor changes the plan. Two days before the

00:01:26.361 --> 00:01:29.212
meeting, they send the call data, the staffing limits and

00:01:29.212 --> 00:01:32.063
the one rule that cannot change. They say the team

00:01:32.063 --> 00:01:34.914
is advising, and the decision will be made Friday. They

00:01:34.914 --> 00:01:37.765
ask each person to bring one pattern they hear on

00:01:37.765 --> 00:01:39.190
calls that others may not.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.824
In the meeting, they go around in a set order,

00:01:42.824 --> 00:01:46.108
starting with those who usually speak last. Structure matters here.

00:01:46.108 --> 00:01:49.393
In the same meta-analysis, structured discussion was linked to more

00:01:49.393 --> 00:01:52.020
sharing and a bigger payoff from unique information.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:56.530
Two workers who take most of the Spanish-language calls describe

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:00.690
a delay that no report shows. Another worker raises a

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.850
problem: the new sort could push urgent callbacks behind routine

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.930
ones. A senior colleague disagrees.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.414
This is the moment that decides whether people keep speaking

00:02:10.414 --> 00:02:13.549
up. Research on voice finds that raising problems is often

00:02:13.549 --> 00:02:16.683
rated lower than offering suggestions, even when the warning is

00:02:16.683 --> 00:02:19.818
right. So the supervisor thanks the worker for the specific

00:02:19.818 --> 00:02:22.952
point, and asks both people to walk through a real

00:02:22.952 --> 00:02:26.087
call. The risk turns out to be real. The plan

00:02:26.087 --> 00:02:26.400
changes.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.859
On Friday, the supervisor sends the final plan with a

00:02:29.859 --> 00:02:32.969
short note. It names what changed, who raised each point,

00:02:32.969 --> 00:02:36.078
and why one popular idea was not used. A review

00:02:36.078 --> 00:02:39.188
of voice research found that people speak up when they

00:02:39.188 --> 00:02:42.297
believe it will have an impact. The note is the

00:02:42.297 --> 00:02:43.230
evidence of impact.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.758
The lesson is simple to say and harder to do.

00:02:46.758 --> 00:02:49.937
Involve people on fewer decisions, and do it fully. Share

00:02:49.937 --> 00:02:53.115
first. Ask for what only they know. Protect the people

00:02:53.115 --> 00:02:55.340
who raise problems. And show what changed.
